131
00:08:46.169 --> 00:08:47.278
Common mistakes.

132
00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:49.637
A fixed window.

133
00:08:49.917 --> 00:08:53.437
Clients can send 2N within a second around each boundary.

134
00:08:53.717 --> 00:08:56.277
Read-then-write in Redis without atomicity.

135
00:08:56.557 --> 00:09:01.144
Use a Lua script or MULTI, or two app servers will each allow the last slot.

136
00:09:01.424 --> 00:09:02.853
Using the client's clock.

137
00:09:03.133 --> 00:09:05.565
Use the server's or Redis's time (TIME).

138
00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:09.600
One global limiter key for all users, which creates a hot key.

139
00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:11.331
Shard by user id.

140
00:09:12.681 --> 00:09:13.577
Part 9.

141
00:09:13.857 --> 00:09:15.201
Follow-up questions.

142
00:09:16.451 --> 00:09:17.325
Question.

143
00:09:17.605 --> 00:09:20.549
How do you rate-limit across 50 gateway instances?

144
00:09:20.829 --> 00:09:22.195
Thirty-second answer:

145
00:09:22.475 --> 00:09:35.296
Use a central store (Redis) with an atomic script per request, or local limiters each with N or instances (cheap, approximate), or local token buckets refilled from a central budget periodically.

146
00:09:35.576 --> 00:09:37.859
Choose by how strictly the limit must hold.

147
00:09:39.209 --> 00:09:40.083
Question.

148
00:09:40.363 --> 00:09:41.985
What should the response look like?

149
00:09:42.265 --> 00:09:43.630
Thirty-second answer:

150
00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:55.110
429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After and RateLimit-Limit or RateLimit-Remaining or RateLimit-Reset headers, so well-behaved clients back off precisely.

